#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065011} :activity/id #uuid "1279e2e9-86d7-5a4e-b13a-2eb99b4a0896" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049270 :topic/id #uuid "e8c80d1f-e431-5719-bd9e-4793fa8d0ec3" :topic/math-academy-id 347} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065436 :tutorial/content "The **intersection** of two intervals is their set of common values, and is denoted by the intersection symbol $\\cap.$\n\nFor example, the intersection of the two intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty)$ is denoted\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)\n$$\n\nand corresponds to the set of values that are in both intervals $(-\\infty, 2]$ and $[-1,\\infty).$\n\nTo represent the intersection on a number line, we can start by sketching the two intervals separately:\n\n![](images/a0/a0b486ac0f981fc5a0161c5eabba9ec257bd0a03e5f0e27397f6fe8e81eb39a5.png)\n\n![](images/8a/8a98f83055b49c14ed08fff658ace9527c5134879a66245aff02e446d44a5c87.png)\n\nThe intersection $(-\\infty, 2] \\cap [-1,\\infty)$ represents the values that are in both intervals. So, it can be graphed by finding where the two intervals overlap:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a5126928d43be82d3e4a167b52b34d8ed4ec33e94b991ecb8486caf3e3d4a9.png)\n\nTherefore, the intersection of the intervals is ${\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.$ Symbolically, we write\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-\\infty,2]} \\cap {\\color{red}[-1,\\infty)} = {\\color{blue}[-1,2]}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "db563ec9-015b-505d-a764-ade8484216bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8683 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "25c811fa-dac2-5b24-9196-30d8ad83117a" :step/math-academy-id 137735 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064173 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063972 :question/id #uuid "02afcfcd-c405-5add-a291-75e6db923e3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13943"} :knowledge-poin